---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What does Tracepod actually do?',
    a: `Tracepod watches a running container with an eBPF kprobe on <code>openat()</code> and
    records every file it actually opens. The <code>harden</code> CLI then takes that
    manifest and builds a new OCI image containing only the files that were observed,
    their recursively resolved ELF shared-library dependencies, and anything you
    explicitly included. The result is a smaller image: fewer files and fewer
    packages than the base image it started from, which means a smaller attack
    surface.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What is NRI, and why does the sensor need it?',
    a: `NRI (Node Resource Interface) is containerd's plugin interface for observing
    container lifecycle events. It's the only mechanism the sensor uses to find out when
    a container starts and stops, so it can attach its eBPF program to the right
    container. NRI ships disabled by default on containerd below version 2.0, and
    enabled by default from 2.0 onward &mdash; see
    <a href="/docs/getting-started/requirements/">Requirements</a> for which node images
    that affects today, and how to turn it on where it's off.`,
  },
  {
    q: "What happens if I start a container with docker run instead of through Kubernetes?",
    a: `It won't be profiled, and nothing tells you that. <code>docker run</code>,
    <code>nerdctl run</code>, <code>docker compose</code>, and <code>ctr run</code> all
    talk to containerd's native API directly rather than going through the CRI plugin,
    so they never fire the NRI events the sensor listens for. The sensor stays healthy,
    the container runs normally, and no manifest is ever written &mdash; there's no error
    on either side. Start the container through kubelet (a normal Pod) or <code>crictl</code>
    instead.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How long do I need to profile, and when does the manifest get written?',
    a: `In standalone mode (the default, no controller configured) a manifest is written
    for a container as soon as that container stops &mdash; there's no fixed profiling
    window you have to wait out. What matters is whether the traffic the container saw
    before it stopped was representative of production: a profile recorded against an
    idle container will be missing files that only get opened under real load.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What does the confidence score mean?',
    a: `It's a coverage signal, not a quality grade: it answers "how representative was
    this profiling session," not "is this image safe." It starts at 100 and loses points
    for things like a short profiling window or manually added paths the sensor never
    observed. A score below 100 doesn't mean the image is broken &mdash; it means specific,
    named gaps are still open, and the build output tells you exactly which ones.
    <strong>The penalty weights are v1 defaults</strong>, calibrated against a reference
    nginx profile, and are expected to change before GA &mdash; don't hard-code specific
    thresholds into CI gates yet. Full breakdown in the
    <a href="/docs/concepts/observation-sources/">confidence scoring docs</a>.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What SBOM formats does it produce?',
    a: `Both CycloneDX and SPDX, generated via <code>syft</code> when you pass
    <code>--sbom</code> to <code>harden build</code>. If you also pass a cosign signing
    key, each SBOM is signed and a <code>.sig</code> sidecar is written next to it.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What license is Tracepod under?',
    a: `AGPL-3.0. Free to self-host. See the
    <a href="https://github.com/tracepod/tracepod/blob/main/LICENSE" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LICENSE file</a>
    in the repository for the full terms.`,
  },
  {
    q: "What's the difference between the open-source core and Platform (Pro)?",
    a: `The open-source core &mdash; the eBPF sensor, the <code>harden</code> image builder,
    the Helm chart, and the GitHub Action &mdash; is everything you need to profile a
    container and produce a hardened image and its SBOM, and it's free. The Platform adds
    a web dashboard across your whole fleet, automated sandbox validation before a build
    ships, an auto-fix loop for validation failures, CVE delta reporting, fleet policy
    and compliance, and more. See <a href="/open-source">Open Source</a> and
    <a href="/platform">Platform</a> for the full breakdown, or <a href="/pricing">Pricing</a>
    for what's included where.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Does it work on EKS, AKS, GKE, or Fargate?',
    a: `It depends on the node image and the pod-admission policy, not the managed
    offering by name. On EKS and AKS, whether NRI is already on depends on the
    containerd version the node image bundles &mdash; current AL2023 and AKS Ubuntu 24.04
    images have it on by default; older images (like AKS Ubuntu 22.04) need it enabled
    manually. GKE <strong>Autopilot</strong> and <strong>AWS Fargate</strong> block the
    sensor outright: Autopilot blocks privileged pods at admission, and Fargate gives you
    no node to run a DaemonSet on at all. GKE Standard isn't covered by that specific
    block, but every cluster needs to admit a <code>privileged</code>, <code>hostPID</code>
    pod regardless of provider. See <a href="/docs/getting-started/requirements/">Requirements</a>
    for the full picture, and run <code>hack/discovery-probe.sh</code> against a real
    node before you commit to a rollout.`,
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="FAQ — Tracepod"
  description="Answers to practical questions about how Tracepod's eBPF sensor works, what it needs, and what's free vs. Platform."
>
  <section class="faq-hero section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="eyebrow">FAQ</div>
      <h1 class="heading-xl">Questions people actually ask.</h1>
      <p class="body-lg" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; max-width: 640px;">
        Straight answers about what the sensor needs, what it misses, and where the free
        tooling ends and the platform begins.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container faq-list-container">
      {faqs.map((item) => (
        <details class="faq-entry">
          <summary class="faq-question">{item.q}</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer" set:html={item.a} />
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band section">
    <div class="container cta-band-inner">
      <div>
        <h2 class="heading-lg">Still have a question?</h2>
        <p class="body-md" style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
          Check the <a href="/docs">docs</a>, or ask us directly.
        </p>
      </div>
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Contact us</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
.faq-hero { padding-top: var(--space-16); padding-bottom: var(--space-4); }

.faq-list-container {
  max-width: 760px;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: var(--space-3);
}

.faq-entry {
  border: 1px solid var(--border);
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  background: var(--bg-elevated);
  padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-5);
}

.faq-question {
  cursor: pointer;
  list-style: none;
  padding: var(--space-3) 0;
  font-size: 1rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--text-primary);
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
  gap: var(--space-4);
}
.faq-question::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
.faq-question::after {
  content: '+';
  font-weight: 400;
  color: var(--text-muted);
  flex-shrink: 0;
  font-size: 1.2rem;
}
.faq-entry[open] .faq-question::after { content: '\2212'; }

.faq-answer {
  padding: 0 0 var(--space-4);
  font-size: 0.9rem;
  line-height: 1.75;
  color: var(--text-secondary);
}
.faq-answer code {
  font-family: var(--font-mono);
  color: var(--accent);
  font-size: 0.85em;
}
.faq-answer a { color: var(--accent); }
.faq-answer strong { color: var(--text-primary); }

.cta-band { background: var(--bg-elevated); border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); margin-top: var(--space-8); }
.cta-band-inner { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: var(--space-8); flex-wrap: wrap; }
.btn-lg { padding: 0.8rem 1.75rem; font-size: 1rem; }
</style>
